        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "9178c819-8c7e-59b8-aecd-b17b003f6e1e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alan Young, Charles Dickens\n- Products: The Alan Young Show, Mickey's Christmas Carol, A Christmas Carol\n- Organizations: Disney, TV Guide, Academy Awards, Emmy Awards\n- Roles: actor, voice actor, comedian, radio host, television host\n\nFacts:\n- Alan Young was born Angus Young on November 19, 1919, and he died on May 19, 2016.\n- Alan Young was a British-born Canadian-American actor, voice actor, comedian, and radio and television host and personality.\n- TV Guide called Alan Young “The Charlie Chaplin of Television.”\n- During the 1940s and 1950s, Alan Young starred in his own variety and comedy sketch program, The Alan Young Show, on radio and television.\n- The television version of The Alan Young Show won Alan Young two Emmy Awards in 1951.\n- Disney’s Mickey's Christmas Carol was released in 1983 and was nominated for an Academy Award.\n- Mickey's Christmas Carol is an adaptation of Charles Dickens’ A Christmas Carol.\n- In Mickey's Christmas Carol, Scrooge McDuck plays the Ebenezer Scrooge role.\n- Alan Young first voiced Disney’s Scrooge McDuck in Mickey's Christmas Carol.\n- Alan Young voiced Scrooge McDuck for more than thirty years in films, television series, and video games until his death.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "2e687b81-d58a-5780-bd32-ead2d74939b8",
        "text": "Page: Alan Young. Sentence: Alan Young (born Angus Young; November 19, 1919 – May 19, 2016) was a British-born Canadian-American actor, voice actor, comedian and radio and television host/personality who \"TV Guide\" called \"The Charlie Chaplin of Television\".\nPage: Alan Young. Sentence: Young was also the voice of Disney's Scrooge McDuck for over thirty years, first in the Academy Award-nominated short film \"Mickey's Christmas Carol\" (1983) and in various other films, TV series and video games until his death.\nPage: Alan Young. Sentence: During the 1940s and 1950s, he starred in his own variety/comedy sketch shows \"The Alan Young Show\" on radio and television, the latter gaining him two Emmy Awards in 1951.\nPage: Mickey's Christmas Carol. Sentence: The cartoon is an adaptation of Charles Dickens' \"A Christmas Carol\", starring Scrooge McDuck as Ebenezer Scrooge.",
